@htp_senate
new-page: true

= The Senate: coalitions and relations

**Relations.** The Caucus has a relationship with every bloc, and every pair of blocs has one with each other: *hostile*, *cold*, *cool*, *cordial*, *warm* or *close*. The Senate tab shows them. Relations drift toward what the blocs stand for and what their leaders think of each other. Governing together warms a pair; running rival candidates for Chancellor cools it.

**Who will govern with whom.** No bloc will sit in a government beside a partner it is too cold toward, and each leader sets the bar: a **hardliner** needs cordial ties, a **firm** leader cool ones, and a **pragmatic** leader will sit even with a bloc they are cold toward (never a hostile one). A leader's mood moves their firmness: anger digs them in, gratitude makes them bend. A new Chancellor builds a coalition in order of their relationship with each party, the Caucus included, until it holds a majority. Every partner in a coalition the Caucus leads must clear the bar with us and with each other, and each names a price: a committee chair, a pledge, or both.

**Mending ties.** The Inter-bloc Relations card courts a bloc, brokers talks between two blocs whose coldness blocks a coalition we could lead, or hosts a summit for a whole coalition. **Delivering** counts for more: meeting a bloc leader's demand, writing a budget that funds what a bloc wants, or keeping a pledge for a full term.

**A Senate that cannot govern.** When no coalition can reach a majority, or a government falls apart (a coalition collapse, or the bureaucrats bringing it down), voters lose faith in the system: Palpatine's bloc gains, separatism rises and faith falls.

**Coalition mood.** A coalition the Caucus sits in grows restless every turn, faster with hardline partners. Concessions, the Coalition Affairs card and some advisors calm it. If its mood reaches *collapsing*, a partner walks out, brings the government down (unless the constructive no-confidence amendment protects the Chancellor), and will not serve with the Caucus for two years. A Caucus Chancellor can also govern as a **minority**, keeping more committee chairs for itself, but it then leans on the blocs outside the government whose votes it needs. Their patience wears out faster than a partner's, every one of the government's bills the Senate votes down tries it further, and the government falls a step sooner.

**Pledges.** A pledge, made in a Chancellor race, wins a bloc but binds the Caucus until the next Chancellor election. Coalition partners ask only for committee chairs, never for promises. Budgets and votes warn before we break one. A broken pledge costs that bloc's goodwill and our momentum, strains a coalition, and its leader holds the line harder against us for years.

**The bureaucrats.** Once the Caucus governs or chairs a committee, the Senate's permanent staff judge us by the partners we choose and the reforms we pass. Cold, they lose our bills in committee; hostile, they build a case against us and strike. Finis Valorum, Bail Organa or Bail Antilles can work with them as advisors, the Judiciary & Intelligence Committee can reform them, and a more establishment coalition reassures them.

**Leaders.** Every bloc and every faction of the Caucus has a leader whose mood follows what they care about and what they remember of us. Now and then a leader makes a demand or an offer.

**Shifting blocs.** Over the years the Core Moderates and the Independents lose about half their natural support. It goes to the Caucus or to Palpatine: more of it to us when our relations with the Moderates are good, our momentum is high, the evidence against Sidious is public and reforms have passed; more to him when he holds the Chancellery and the Senate is corrupt and afraid. The Independents also lose voters to the Separatists where separatism runs high, and to the Rimward Coalition where the Rim suffers. The Security Bloc drifts toward Palpatine while he holds power or fear is high.

**Defections.** A bloc or faction under a hardline leader may be subverted. The Rimward Coalition (under Ardana), our own Rimward wing (under Corvane) or the Rim Freedom Caucus may go over to the Separatists; the Security Bloc, led by Tarkin and cold toward us, may fold into Palpatine's bloc. A warning comes first, and the next leadership challenge comes sooner. In the few turns that follow, the threat passes only if relations with that bloc reach *cordial* (for our own wing, if its anger cools), if the Caucus takes a reform step that answers its grievances (the Rim, slavery or the syndicates for the Rim; the military for the Security Bloc), or if a rival we backed unseats the hardliner. Otherwise it happens, and after Geonosis the Rim's defectors leave the Republic altogether.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_elections
new-page: true

= Elections and campaigns

**Momentum** lifts our support everywhere and sways every bloc on election day. Reforms, strong campaigns, debates, endorsements and won contests build it; scandals, gaffes, broken promises and lost contests drain it. It fades each quarter unless renewed.

**Campaigning.** A campaign picks a region and a message. *Clean government* needs a corrupt Senate and clean hands; *Rim relief* needs hardship and a record of funding relief; *security* needs fear; *peace* lands with a war-weary galaxy but backfires when people are afraid; *the Republic* is steady everywhere. A campaign's gains in a region last for years and fade slowly; campaigning there again builds on them, though each time adds less. In the last year before a Chancellor election the Caucus can campaign every turn, and the campaign trail brings debates, endorsements, opposition research, gaffes and smears.

**The map.** The Polls tab's map of the galaxy shades each region by the bloc that leads it; hover over a region, or tap it, to see its delegations. The same map appears at each new session of the Senate and at Chancellor elections.

**Rallies** can be stormed. The Judicial Forces, a reformed Senate Guard, or local security where we are popular can protect them; each use of armed force troubles the Pacifists.

**The Chancellor race.** Our nominee appeals to some blocs more than others, and so do the rivals. The whip count is reliable with the Judiciary chair, strong evidence or Bail Organa or Finis Valorum as an advisor. If no one wins the first ballot, the corridors between the ballots become a marketplace.

**Across the galaxy.** World elections move delegations and momentum. Once Dooku's movement is under way, worlds vote on leaving the Republic. Regions angered by measures we back (levies, a debased credit, relief cuts, tariffs, a starved war effort) may recall Caucus senators.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_purse
new-page: true

= The Republic's purse

**The budget.** Once a year the Republic needs a budget, written by whoever holds the Chancellery or the Finance chair, and it must pass the Senate. Every source of money has people who pay for it, and they push back harder each year it is used in a row. Two of our own budgets defeated in a row bring down a Caucus Chancellor.

**The guilds' patience** wears down with tariffs, regulation and seizures and recovers with concessions, subsidies and lifted sanctions. As it falls they escalate, step by step.

**Our Core patrons** fund the Caucus. Angry, they cut our yearly political capital and our support in the Core erodes.

**Guild money.** Taking money from guild donors, in Patron Contributions or as a war chest in a Chancellor race, raises corruption, and while we owe them the guilds block every reform that would hurt them: the ethics law and the integrity office, guild regulation, breaking up the Trade Federation, the Banking Clan's independence, and tariffs in our budgets. Breaking with them (in Patron Contributions) costs political capital and their goodwill, and they may leak what we took.

**Debt and markets.** The more the Republic owes, the more the Banking Clan can ask; repaying it, or guaranteeing the Clan's independence, loosens its grip. While Tibanna is scarce, defence costs more.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_war
new-page: true

= The Separatists and the war

**The Separatist cause** grows every year from 31 BBY, faster in the Outer and Mid Rim, unless the Caucus deals with the Rim's grievances: the Rim Relief Act, enfranchisement and above all a Rim Assembly; abolishing slavery and enforcing it; breaking the syndicates; and relief for the Rim, in budgets and on the ground. The Forces tab shows whether the cause is gathering strength or held back. In wartime it also feeds the droid armies.

**War aims and the fronts.** The Senate sets the war aims, shown on the Forces tab. Every second turn the War Council decides where the Republic commits its strength. Each front feeds a different part of the galaxy: losing the Outer Rim Sieges brings hunger and separatism to the Rim, losing the Hydian Way strangles trade, and losing the Core approaches brings fear to Coruscant.

**Named worlds.** While the Confederacy is stronger than the Republic, or a front is collapsing, its armies break through and take loyalist worlds one by one, from the edge of the war toward the Core: each loss raises fear, erodes faith in the Republic and in the Jedi, and costs the Senate delegations. A stronger Republic, advancing, takes them back. If the Core falls as far as Alderaan, Coruscant itself is next.

**The Republic's strength** comes from the clone army, the Judicial Forces, the member worlds' forces, the Jedi and a loyal Navy; a Senate-commanded army and a New Jedi Order add to it, and so does a deal with the Hutts.

**The Sith Lord's hand.** While Palpatine rules the Republic, he keeps its war from being lost: he needs it long, not lost. Out of office, he no longer holds the droid armies back, and strength counts for more. Once he is captured or killed, the two sides' real strength alone decides the war.

**How the war stands.** The Forces tab sums up the war in a word: being won, in the balance, or being lost. It counts the fronts, the two sides' strength and the worlds already taken. An event warns you when the front gives way.

**After Sidious.** Capturing or killing the Sith Lord does not end the war. If the war is being lost when the game ends, the Republic falls to the Confederacy, even with Sidious gone. A civil war fought while the war is being lost ends the same way. If Sidious is exposed, or beaten on Coruscant, while the war is going against the Republic, he may escape to the Confederacy and make it his own: then the Republic must be winning clearly to survive.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_sith
new-page: true

= The search for the Sith

The Caucus does not know who the Sith Lord is. It has to find out. The Shadow tab shows how far the search has come.

**Stages.** The search moves through five stages, each needing the one before: *rumours*; *a Sith presence* (the Jedi confirm one has returned); *a hidden hand* (one pattern behind the crises); *close to power* (the Sith Lord is on Coruscant, near the top); and *a name*. Evidence (shown as *Our proof*) moves the search on, but each stage also needs a clue: the Damask trail, Gunray's testimony, the Kamino payments, a Bureau intelligence review, the Labyrinth raid, the Jedi's growing suspicion.

**The shortlist.** Close to power, the Caucus has four suspects: Mas Amedda, Sate Pestage, Orn Free Taa and Lott Dod. *Look into* each one (Shadow Investigation), use a Bureau intelligence review, arrest the Vice Chair or raid the Labyrinth to clear them. Each is guilty of something, but not of this. When the last is cleared, the Caucus has its name. A turned Count Dooku would name him at once.

**Mas Amedda's files** can be leaked once he is cleared: he loses the gavel and the Senate's staff are pleased, but the Sith Lord learns how close we are.

**Knowing is not proving.** With the name, the Caucus can lay charges, prepare a warrant and open *The Hunt for Darth Sidious*: turn an informant, freeze his money, name compromised officers, find the clone order, watch the ways off Coruscant, bring the Jedi in, hide the witnesses, tell Anakin, or build the public case. Each needs its groundwork, and the riskier ones raise **his alarm**. At *He knows we are close*, he brings his plans forward by a year. At *He is moving*, he strikes at once.

**If the Caucus holds the Chancellery**, it can order his arrest. He may flee to the Confederacy (unless the ways off Coruscant are watched), be taken, or fight the arrest with everyone who still answers to him, forcing the final battle for Coruscant. The groundwork counts: a warrant, the Judicial Forces, the Jedi, conclusive proof, an informant, and Anakin above all.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_government
new-page: true

= The government

The Senate's work is done in **six committees**, and whoever chairs one decides what it puts to the Senate. Holding a chair puts that committee's card in the *Senate Affairs* deck.

• **Finance & Commerce:** the budget, the guilds and the banks.

• **Judiciary & Intelligence:** the courts, corruption, the bureaucracy and the Bureau of Intelligence.

• **Security & Defence:** the army, the Navy, the Senate Guard and the pirates.

• **Outer Rim Affairs:** relief, slavery and the Rim's representation.

• **Constitutional Affairs:** amendments, the Chancellor's office and the emergency powers.

• **Jedi Affairs:** the Jedi Order's place in the Republic, and its future.

**Sharing out the chairs.** Every new government shares the chairs out afresh. Each partner in the coalition insists on at least one, and a firm leader insists on the one it cares about most. The Caucus buys the rest with its leverage, which comes from its seats: Finance & Commerce costs 20, Judiciary, Security and the Rim office 10 each, and Constitutional and Jedi Affairs 5 each. In opposition, the Caucus chairs nothing.

**Ministers.** Every chair is held by a named senator. When the Caucus takes a chair, it chooses who sits in it from its own senators, and the faction that senator belongs to is pleased. The committees the Caucus does not chair are not idle: every few turns each one acts on the course of the bloc that holds it, as that bloc's leader sees things at the time. A guild-friendly Finance committee cuts regulation, a Rimward one sends relief, and one in Palpatine's hands quietly smooths his way. Some of those acts are reforms put to the Senate, though the Core Moderates and the Independents only put reforms forward when their reforming wings lead them. *The Committees Report* tells you what they did, and the Senate tab shows who chairs each committee.

**The Chancellery.** The Chancellor's office is not a committee: it is an office of its own, and it gathers powers. It starts with the gavel and the Senate Guard, takes over the committees' staff in time, and gains more with each emergency: decrees, the Grand Army, the Bureau of Intelligence, the HoloNet, the banks, the sector governors and others. A reform can check each power for good. The more powers the office holds, the more weight its holder's own bills carry, and under Palpatine every power feeds his plan. The pinned *The Chancellery* card shows the office, its powers and the government; looking costs nothing.

**Rule by decree.** While our own Chancellor holds the office and it holds emergency decrees, the Chancellery card can enact a committee's next reform without a Senate vote, or use one of the office's powers. Each decree takes the turn and sets a precedent: faith in the Republic slowly falls, the Restoration grows harder, and a later Palpatine inherits it all. Rule by decree often enough while the office keeps its powers, and the game ends as *The Hollow Crown*. Our Chancellor can also hand a power back to the Senate for good. Other Chancellors with emergency decrees rule by decree on their own every so often.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_reforms
new-page: true

= Reforms, step by step

Many of the Republic's problems can only be fixed in several steps, each a separate decision and most a Senate vote. Each committee has its own paths, and the Status tab shows how far each has gone. Each step needs the one before it.

• **Finance & Commerce.** *The guilds:* regulating them, then the Anti-Cartel Act, then breaking up the Trade Federation, which weakens the guilds' bloc and ends every guild threat for good. *The banks:* an audit of the Banking Clan, then its independence from the Chancellery, then Republic bonds, which cut the Republic's interest. The committee can also cut regulation, as often as it likes: the guilds and trade gain, the treasury and the Rim lose, and grateful donors fill the Caucus's coffers. Three rounds of it and the guilds' security forces will answer the Caucus in the Last Days.

• **Judiciary & Intelligence.** *Corruption:* the ethics law, then an Office of Public Integrity, then expelling the Senate's most corrupt members. *The bureaucracy:* a civil service review, then the Civil Service Act, after which the bureaucrats are no longer a threat and Vice Chair Mas Amedda can be arrested. *Intelligence:* an intelligence review, then the Intelligence Oversight Act, then the Commission for the Protection of the Republic, which sees schemes coming, keeps the proof from wasting away and protects investigators.

• **Security & Defence.** *Command of the army:* Senate oversight, then the Senate Command Act (the Senate Guard must be reformed first). *The Senate's guard:* returning the Guard to the Senate, then the Judicial Forces Charter. *The syndicates:* patrol fleets in the Rim (after investing in the military), a campaign against the pirate fleets (the fleets must be strong enough, or the Republic is humiliated), then breaking the syndicates (Judiciary). Until something is done, pirates raid the trade lanes.

• **Outer Rim Affairs.** *The Rim:* the Rim Relief Act, then enfranchisement, then a Rim Assembly, which stops the Separatist cause from growing. *Slavery:* a Senate report, abolition, then enforcing it, which needs the syndicates broken. Each step helps the Caucus on the Rim and Anakin's state of mind; enforcement frees his mother.

• **Constitutional Affairs.** *Emergency powers:* sunset clauses, then the Powers Review Act, which takes one power back every year. *The office of Chancellor:* entrenched term limits and a constructive vote of no confidence (in either order), then an independent Speaker, which ends the Vice Chair's control of the agenda. Amendments need two-thirds of the Senate (three-fifths when faith is high).

• **Jedi Affairs.** *The Order's future:* a campaign for reform (the Caucus's own Jedi card can speak for it too), then a Council that accepts attachment, then a New Jedi Order, which needs Anakin Skywalker firmly on the light side and close relations with the Order. *The Order's independence:* the Jedi Independence Amendment, then the Jedi Service Act (the Jedi serve as peacekeepers, never as generals: a standing law, which the hawks may try to suspend when war comes), then a Senate–Jedi Compact.

• **High Human Culture**, across the committees: the Rights of Sentience (Rim office), a diverse officer corps (Security) and an investigation of the human-first groups (Judiciary) together discredit it. Until then it keeps rising, helping Palpatine and the Separatists and making hardline leaders likelier.

- @how_to_play: Back to How to play

@htp_dangers
new-page: true

= Dangers and warnings

• **The New Order, early.** If the Chancellor holds 8 emergency powers while the Senate's resolve is below 5, the next crisis lets him proclaim the Empire.

• **The Senatorial Arrests.** Senate resolve of 5, or a Navy at least 60% loyal to the Senate, makes him think twice.

• **The admirals.** With fear at 60 or more, Tarkin leading the Security Bloc and the Navy's loyalty below 50%, the Security Bloc may seize power. A loyal Navy or a reformed Senate Guard stops them.

• **The admirals, later.** Even after Palpatine is arrested or killed, if the Navy's loyalty is below 50%, emergency powers remain and High Human Culture has not been discredited, the admirals may take the Republic for themselves.

• **The Sith Confederacy.** If Sidious is exposed or escapes while the Confederacy is winning the war, he may flee to it. If the Republic then loses the war, he rules the galaxy from Raxus.

• **The Fall of the Republic.** A Confederacy far stronger than the Republic, or one that reaches Coruscant, conquers it, whatever has become of the Sith Lord.

• **Schemes.** If we are watching closely, we get a turn's warning before a scheme lands.

• **The Rim leaves by default.** Rim separatism grows whenever Rim hardship is above 20 or corruption above 40, faster the further above. Left alone, the Rim is ready to break by the time Dooku speaks on Raxus.

• **The Rim's politicians radicalise.** Each year from 28 BBY without action on slavery or Rim enfranchisement, our Rimward wing and the Rimward Coalition move a step closer to Dooku. At the last step, radicals lead both, and they are ready to defect as soon as he declares.

• **Anakin falls unless something holds him.** From month 96 the dark side rises under him, and by month 168 nothing pulls him back. Only three things stop it: freeing his mother; a Jedi Order reformed to accept attachment; or all of keeping the Jedi out of command, a warning about Palpatine that was heard, and action on slavery. The *People* panel shows what holds him, under Persons of interest. A fallen Anakin betrays the arrest and fights for the Sith Lord.

• **The arrest weighs the forces.** Everyone who would answer to him is set against everyone who would answer to us. If he is clearly stronger, even a well-prepared arrest can fail.

• **The Last Days only draw on what you built.** Each Last Days card can be played once, and needs its groundwork: mobilising the Free Worlds needs the Defence Pact; appealing to the Navy needs earlier work with its officers (vetting them on Security & Defence, a diversified officer corps, or the Senate Command Act); rallying the Delegation needs the Delegation of 2000; securing the evidence needs a case (it protects the evidence, it does not add to it); coordinating with the Jedi needs close relations with the Order; and reaching Anakin needs someone close to him: Padmé, Jedi friends or Qui-Gon. An unreformed Senate Guard cannot be won over. At the arrest, only a warrant prepared earlier counts. When Palpatine makes his move, the Defence Pact and the army can only be called on if they exist and answer to the Senate.

